  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="origin-story" id="why-seedmask">
        <p class="eyebrow">Origin</p>
        <h2>Why was SeedMask born?</h2>
        <p class="origin-lead">
          Having crypto is the easy part. <strong>Maintaining it</strong> — storing seeds safely, tracking accounts, remembering passphrases and derivation details — is <strong>the real struggle</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="origin-prose">
          <p>
            One seed is hard enough. <strong>Multiple seeds</strong>, multiple accounts under each, <strong>passphrases</strong> on top of those — and suddenly you are not only securing coins. You are securing a map: which <strong>index</strong>, which <strong>derivation path</strong>, which passphrase belongs where.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where do you put that map? In a <strong>book</strong> — someone finds it, or water and time destroy it. On a <strong>phone</strong> — malware and backups. On a “clean” spare phone — you still do not truly know what runs on it. On an <strong>air-gapped laptop</strong> writing to USB — the machine can still leave traces you never intended. Invent your own <strong>cipher alphabet</strong> — easy to forget; write the rules down — easy for someone else to reverse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="origin-almost">
          <h3>The almost-solution</h3>
          <p>
            There was a path that looked serious on paper: encrypted keypad USB, forever-offline phone, point-and-shoot camera with MicroSD, standalone CD duplicator, portable CD/DVD player, printer with USB — then <strong>split each 24-word seed into quarters</strong> across those media. First six words photographed to MicroSD. Next six onto the encrypted stick and burned to disc. Next six from an air-gapped editor onto ordinary USBs. Last six written, scanned, copied, printer wiped. <strong>Multiple copies. Multiple hiding places.</strong> A ritual to reassemble when restore day came.
          </p>
          <p>
            It felt complete — until you lived with it. <strong>Too many parts. Too many locations.</strong> Heat, moisture, and electrostatic discharge waiting to <strong>corrupt</strong> MicroSD, USB, and discs. SeedQR helps portability, but unprotected (or even “protected”) media still get <strong>lost, damaged, or stolen</strong> as physical objects.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="origin-need">
          <p class="origin-need-label">What was actually needed</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Something that does not silently rot</li>
            <li>Physical and portable</li>
            <li>Easy to recover when life goes wrong</li>
            <li>Highly secure</li>
            <li>Writable — a real backup you can hold</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="origin-prose">
          <p>
            Hiring that into existence costs real money and rare trust. The other path: <strong>build it yourself</strong>. The idea of how it must work was already clear — even before the code. With AI and many iterations, <strong>SeedMask took shape</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The core bargain is simple and strict: a writable <strong>backup code</strong> plus <strong>your own password</strong> always derives your seed the same way. <strong>No maybe. No lucky guesses.</strong> Strong password discipline and careful password storage are still on you — but the recovery path is <strong>deterministic</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can carry the backup code. Leave it in a drawer. Entrust it to someone careful — or even someone you do not fully trust — <em>if</em> the password is strong enough that <strong>the code alone is useless</strong>. That is not a license to be reckless with photos and printouts. It is a measure of how the design is meant to work: <strong>the backup code is portable; the password is the gate</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="origin-close">
          SeedMask was born because the DIY vault stack was <strong>too fragile for real life</strong> — and self-custody deserved something <strong>physical, recoverable</strong>, and honest about where the risk still sits.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="name-story">
        <p class="eyebrow">The name</p>
        <h2>Why “SeedMask”</h2>
        <div class="name-story-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>It masks your seed</h3>
            <p>
              Your seed phrase is the master key to your funds. SeedMask is designed so that seed never leaves the device — in the final hardware form, the plan is that it stays masked inside SeedMask and never gets out onto a computer, phone, or the network.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Safer to store, easier to live with</h3>
            <p>
              Masking the seed is not only about keeping it offline. It is about storing it better, safer, and more conveniently — so you can use Bitcoin and Kaspa without writing secrets into everyday apps or leaving recovery words exposed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="name-story-note">
          At first, SeedMask can sound dangerous or even contradictory — as if something were being hidden from you. That is far from the truth. The name is a promise of protection: SeedMask aims to become a wallet that guards your seed, generates it safely and with high entropy, and keeps it masked from the places that leak — so the product can live up to what it is called.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container about-grid">
      <article class="about-card">
        <span class="pillar-index">Vision</span>
        <h3>Keys stay on SeedMask</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Keys on device. Watch-only on desktop. Signing stays offline.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          SeedMask is the vault you hold: seed generation, storage, and signing stay on the hardware — masked from phones, browsers, and everyday machines that were never built to keep a seed safe.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="about-card">
        <span class="pillar-index">Focus</span>
        <h3>Bitcoin &amp; Kaspa only</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Satoshi’s vision. Two coins means clearer signing screens and a smaller attack surface than a multi-coin wallet.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Every extra chain adds formats, fees, and confirmation edge cases at the moment you should be careful. SeedMask stays narrow on purpose — Bitcoin and Kaspa done properly, not a crowded catalog of half-supported assets.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="about-card">
        <span class="pillar-index">Stance</span>
        <h3>100% open-source</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Firmware, Coordinator, and the full signing path — published so anyone can audit it.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Closed wallets ask you to trust a black box. SeedMask is built so independent eyes can inspect how keys are handled, how updates ship, and how the air-gapped path actually works — transparency as a custody requirement, not a slogan.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

