      <div class="tamper-risks" id="physical-security">
        <p class="eyebrow">Hardware</p>
        <h3>Physical security — the retail plan</h3>
        <p>
          These protections are planned for the retail device. They are not in the hardware preview board. This is the plan for the case, the chip, and the board.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Secure element</strong>
            Keys live in a dedicated chip, not a general MCU that is easy to read. Signing stays inside that chip.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Anti-tamper</strong>
            Sensors, seals, and wipe-on-open so opening the case is not a quiet operation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Physical access</strong>
            If the device is left alone, the design still assumes someone can hold the box. The enclosure and chip are there to raise the cost of that, not to ignore it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Supply chain</strong>
            Retail units from the official channel, with packaging and checks that make a swap harder to hide.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Chip-off / decap</strong>
            Sensitive material is meant to stay in protected silicon — not in a chip a lab can lift and dump.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Fault injection / glitching</strong>
            The secure element and board design make voltage, clock, and EM tricks harder while the device looks closed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Side channels</strong>
            Hardening so power, EM, and timing leak less while you sign or unlock.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Clean production boards</strong>
            No leftover factory debug pads on retail units.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Complete wipe</strong>
            A wipe clears the seed, backups, and debug leftovers together — so nothing useful remains for someone who opens the device later.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Power and batteries</strong>
            Defined behavior when power dies or a battery fails — not a surprise wipe or a dead sensor that looks “safe.”
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Accidental wipe control</strong>
            Thresholds so a drop or a hot day is not treated the same as an opened case.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Signed firmware</strong>
            Physical protections sit on top of firmware you can verify. The case does not replace hash checks and signed updates.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="tamper-risks-close">
          These are planned for retail hardware. They raise the cost of a physical attack. They do not replace confirming on the screen, or keeping keys on the device.
        </p>
      </div>

